= Enzyme — Technical =

== Elementary catalytic cycle ==
A simple schematic for one enzyme is:

<pre>
E + S <=> ES -> E + P
</pre>

E is enzyme, S substrate, ES enzyme–substrate complex, and P product. This illustrative scheme suppresses reverse reactions, intermediates, product inhibition and multiple-substrate mechanisms. Real enzyme mechanisms can be much more elaborate.

== Rates and saturation ==
Under restrictive steady-state, single-substrate Michaelis–Menten assumptions:

<pre>
v = Vmax [S] / (Km + [S])
</pre>

v is the initial rate, [S] substrate concentration, Vmax the limiting rate at saturation, and Km a concentration parameter; Km is not always equal to a dissociation constant. Cooperative, multisubstrate and allosteric enzymes may not obey this simple model.

== Thermodynamic distinction ==
Enzymatic catalysis alters kinetic barriers and both forward and reverse pathways but not the equilibrium constant K at fixed temperature. At equilibrium, there is no net reaction flux even though molecular transitions continue. A rate enhancement should therefore not be interpreted as a change in equilibrium favorability.

== Evidence and controls ==
Kinetic characterization must specify assay conditions, substrate and product detection, background rates, enzyme preparation, replicates and assumptions. Catalytic activity in vitro is not automatically proof of the same physiological reaction or cellular localization.

